**Clarify:** a malloc/free pair over a fixed pool (a static array or one big block from the OS), single-threaded to start, returning aligned pointers.

**Design: implicit free list with block headers.**
```c
typedef struct Block { size_t size; int free; struct Block* next; } Block;
static char pool[1<<20];
static Block* head;

#define ALIGN 16
#define ROUND_UP(n) (((n) + ALIGN - 1) & ~(size_t)(ALIGN - 1))

void init(void){ head=(Block*)pool; head->size=sizeof pool - sizeof(Block); head->free=1; head->next=NULL; }

void* my_alloc(size_t n){
    n = ROUND_UP(n);
    for (Block* b=head; b; b=b->next)
        if (b->free && b->size >= n) {                 /* first fit */
            if (b->size >= n + sizeof(Block) + ALIGN) {  /* split */
                Block* r=(Block*)((char*)(b+1)+n);
                r->size=b->size-n-sizeof(Block); r->free=1; r->next=b->next;
                b->size=n; b->next=r;
            }
            b->free=0; return b+1;
        }
    return NULL;
}
```
`my_free(p)`: get the header with `(Block*)p - 1`, mark free, then **coalesce** with the next block (and the previous one, which needs a back pointer or footer) to fight external fragmentation. Validate `p` (null, inside the pool, not already free).

Points to cover: the `(n + 15) & ~15` rounding keeps every payload aligned (as long as the header size is also a multiple of the alignment, so pad `Block`); first fit versus best fit versus next fit; internal versus external fragmentation; O(n) search (improve with segregated free lists or size classes).

**Alternatives:** a bump/arena allocator (fastest, frees everything at once), a fixed-size pool allocator with a free stack (O(1), no fragmentation), and a buddy allocator (power-of-two blocks, easy coalescing). For threads, add a mutex or per-thread arenas.
